  "slug": "transformer-topic-4726",
  "summary": "Sequence-only and graph-based models now predict binding affinity, host-pathogen interactions and interaction sites from pretrained embeddings.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is learned representations of biomolecules repurposed for interaction and structure tasks. Two lines dominate. The first is graph neural networks operating on sequence, structure or network data, as in ApexPPI's multi-task hyperbolic architecture for host-pathogen interactions and EGCPPIS's hierarchical residue- and atom-level graphs with E(n)-equivariant layers for interaction site identification. The second is pretrained embeddings, where ProtT-Affinity feeds ProtT5 representations into a lightweight Transformer for sequence-only binding affinity. CIRPIN extends graph-based embeddings toward permutation invariance through synthetic circular permutations. CONFIDE adds CODE, an unsupervised metric derived from AlphaFold3 diffusion embeddings, to pLDDT for assessing structure predictions. The newer elements are architectural choices tailored to specific biological invariances and to reliability assessment, alongside prediction itself.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
